{
  "flashcards": [
    {
      "front": "What is the primary difference between a Star Schema and a Snowflake Schema?",
      "back": "A Star Schema denormalizes dimension tables into a single table per dimension, simplifying queries. A Snowflake Schema further normalizes dimension tables into multiple related tables, reducing data redundancy but increasing join complexity.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r3"]
    },
    {
      "front": "What are the four key components of a Fact table in dimensional modeling?",
      "back": "Fact tables contain foreign keys to dimension tables, degenerate dimensions (like invoice numbers), additive/semi-additive/non-additive measures (metrics), and timestamps for time-series analysis.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r3"]
    },
    {
      "front": "How do you handle late-arriving data in a production pipeline?",
      "back": "Use watermarking or buffer tables to hold data until the late records arrive, or design idempotent pipelines that can re-process historical partitions without duplicating data.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r4"]
    },
    {
      "front": "What does it mean for a data pipeline task to be 'idempotent'?",
      "back": "An idempotent task produces the same result regardless of how many times it is executed. This is critical for production pipelines to ensure data integrity during retries or failures.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r4"]
    },
    {
      "front": "Explain the difference between a DAG and a Task in Airflow.",
      "back": "A DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) is the collection of all tasks you want to run, organized in a way that reflects their relationships and dependencies. A Task is the specific unit of work (e.g., a Python script or SQL query) within that DAG.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r5"]
    },
    {
      "front": "How does Dagster differ from Airflow in terms of data awareness?",
      "back": "Dagster is 'data-aware,' meaning it treats data assets as first-class citizens. It focuses on the dependencies between data assets rather than just the execution of tasks, allowing for better lineage and observability.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r6"]
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the purpose of a 'SCD Type 2' in dimensional modeling?",
      "back": "Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2 tracks historical changes by adding new rows for each update, using effective start and end dates to maintain a complete history of attribute changes.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r3"]
    },
    {
      "front": "Why use Python context managers (the 'with' statement) in data engineering?",
      "back": "Context managers ensure that resources like database connections or file handles are properly closed and cleaned up, even if an exception occurs, preventing memory leaks and connection exhaustion.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r1"]
    },
    {
      "front": "What is a 'Common Table Expression' (CTE) and why use it in SQL?",
      "back": "A CTE is a temporary result set defined within a query using the WITH clause. It improves readability, allows for recursive queries, and helps break down complex logic into modular, manageable steps.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r2"]
    },
    {
      "front": "How do you optimize a slow-running SQL query?",
      "back": "Check the execution plan, ensure appropriate indexes exist on join/filter columns, avoid SELECT *, minimize subqueries, and ensure join conditions use indexed columns.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r2"]
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the difference between a 'backfill' and a 'catchup' in Airflow?",
      "back": "Catchup is an automatic Airflow feature that runs missing DAG runs for past dates when a DAG is enabled. A backfill is a manual operation to process historical data for a specific date range.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r5"]
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the benefit of using Python decorators in data pipelines?",
      "back": "Decorators allow you to wrap functions (like tasks) with reusable logic, such as logging, error handling, retries, or performance monitoring, without modifying the core function code.",
      "requirement_ids": ["r1"]
    }
  ]
}
